WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.290
SPEAKER 1: Our speaker
tonight is Lee Habeeb.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:09.470
Mr. Habeeb is vice president
of content at Sales

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.860
Media Group, which syndicates
national talk shows hosted

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.610
by Hugh Hewitt, Dennis
Prager, Larry Elder, Sebastian

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.790
Gorka, Mike Gallagher,
and Eric Metaxas.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.550
A University of Virginia
School of Law graduate,

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.640
Mr. Habeeb is the founder
of American Private Radio

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.270
and host of its storytelling
show Our American Stories,

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.930
a weekday radio show syndicated
across the United States.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.760
He is a weekly
columnist for Newsweek,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.080
and he has written for
numerous other publications,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.730
including National Review,
The Federalist, and LifeZette.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.190
He is speaking this evening
about understanding America

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.710
through its stories.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.107
Please join me in
welcoming Lee Habeeb.

00:00:47.107 --> 00:00:49.095
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:53.575 --> 00:00:54.825
LEE HABEEB: Thank you so much.

00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:57.070
And before I start on
the formal presentation,

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.250
just a story because
the power of story

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.458
is what we're going to really
be talking about today.

00:01:01.458 --> 00:01:03.347
I have a daughter, who
in the seventh grade--

00:01:03.347 --> 00:01:04.930
was about to go into
the eighth grade.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.430
And she had a boyfriend.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.380
The boyfriend was somebody she
went to get ice cream with.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.560
That was it-- with
adult supervision--

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.430
and he was going
into high school.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:14.805
And so one day--
it was Saturday.

00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:16.847
And it was the day she
would go to get ice cream.

00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:18.130
And I would sit in the corner.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.672
And she said, dad, I don't feel
like getting ice cream today.

00:01:20.672 --> 00:01:22.000
Why don't we go to the movies?

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.940
And I'm thinking, hm,
what's going on, sweetheart?

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.410
She goes, I'm not going out
with my boyfriend anymore.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.380
I said, why not?

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.897
She goes, I broke up with him.

00:01:30.897 --> 00:01:32.730
I said, well, why did
you break up with him?

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.260
She goes, well, he's
going to high school.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.320
And he's going to go
there, and he's just

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.653
going to break up with me,
so I broke up with him first.

00:01:37.653 --> 00:01:38.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.260
So this is a preemptive breakup.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.720
And so I thought-- and I did
this in front of my wife.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.178
And every once in a while when
I'm telling stories in front

00:01:46.178 --> 00:01:47.527
of my wife, my wife says this--

00:01:47.527 --> 00:01:48.610
husbands here can attest--

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.410
what are you thinking?

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.090
But I shared some of
my life experience.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:53.507
I said, you know,
sweetheart, when

00:01:53.507 --> 00:01:55.780
I was a junior my
girlfriend was a senior.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.280
And she was going
off to Rutgers.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.110
And I was really afraid.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.420
And so I preemptively
broke up with her.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.317
One Saturday night I
said to my dad, dad,

00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:04.275
let's go to the movies.

00:02:04.275 --> 00:02:05.980
And my father said,
what's up, son?

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.500
And we had the
identical conversation.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.570
Only when I told my
dad I had preemptively

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:14.182
broken up with my girlfriend,
he said, you're such a loser.

00:02:14.182 --> 00:02:15.890
I'm not going out to
the movies with you.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.790
So now I felt terrible,
so I called up

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:21.790
[? Donna ?] [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.140
I remember it like
it's yesterday.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:23.740
I was crying.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.850
I said, [? Donna, ?]
I'm so sorry.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:26.350
I love you.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.173
I was just so afraid you were
going to go off to college

00:02:28.173 --> 00:02:29.320
and break up with me.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.790
Would you take me back?

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:32.283
And she did.

00:02:32.283 --> 00:02:34.450
And so my little girl said,
what happened next, dad?

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.450
I said, well, she went
to Rutgers and dumped me.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.042
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.530
Not sure why I told you that
story, but figured I'd share.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.050
So to start any talk
on storytelling,

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.570
it's best to start with Plato.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:53.110
Those who tell the stories
rule society, he said.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.630
He was right then,
and he's right now.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.120
We know that the most
important book ever written

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.050
in world history and the
biggest blockbuster of all time,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:06.940
the Bible, is nothing but
an endless series of stories

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.620
and some pretty damn good ones--

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.690
Adam and Eve, Cain and
Abel, the prodigal son.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.390
And some people
think the OJ trial

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.940
may have been the
trial of the century,

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.670
but the trial of Jesus
Christ before the Sanhedrin

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.710
may have been the most
important in world history.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.740
The fact is, the four
gospels and their parables

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:31.190
transformed history
and changed the world.

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.320
Indeed, that's what's so
powerful about the Bible

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.320
when you think about it.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.010
Human beings across the
globe have for centuries

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:42.410
been stepping into those stories
and seeing ourselves in them.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.020
For me, it was
the story of Jonah

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.610
that had the most
meaning in my life.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.430
We all have at
least one story we

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.630
can point to in the Bible
that draws us closer to God

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.720
and to each other.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.720
Stories shape reality
in human beings,

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.780
explained the great Brooklyn
novelist Paul Auster.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.260
And the poet Muriel Rukeyser
famously said these words.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:09.430
"The world is made up
of stories, not atoms."

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:12.160
If they are all
correct, why don't we,

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.920
who care about our
nation's story,

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.017
take the business of
storytelling more seriously?

00:04:18.017 --> 00:04:19.600
It's odd, because
we take the business

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.880
of every other business
in American life

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.730
seriously except the
most important one.

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.700
The left has invested
vast fortunes

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.040
in storytelling
pipelines and fill them

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.200
with stories that
shape what Americans

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.940
think about everything--

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.270
business, family, government,
capitalism, our history,

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:38.860
even God.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.690
To understand how
we let this happen,

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:44.120
we must first understand
why we let it happen.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.200
First, we really don't
think storytelling matters.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.240
How do I know?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.460
How many of us would be
happy if a child of ours

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.090
told us they wanted to
be an actor or a writer?

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.910
Now if that same child told us
they wanted to join the Marine

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.930
Corps, I know how we'd react.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.286
We'd be proud.

00:05:02.286 --> 00:05:05.600
At NYU's Tisch School of
the Arts a few years ago,

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.200
Robert De Niro gave the
commencement speech.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.280
He told a few jokes about his
craft, and then he got serious.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.993
He implored the graduates to
work hard and be fearless.

00:05:15.993 --> 00:05:17.410
He reminded them
that they're part

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.560
of an important
tribe called artists

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.080
and that their work
matters and shapes

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.540
the life of their country.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:29.890
De Niro spoke to them like
they were new Army recruits.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.410
That's because the left views
careers in the arts the way

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:35.740
we view careers in the
military and business.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.870
They believe in this stuff.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.220
We don't.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.130
Here's the second reason
we failed up till now.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:43.960
Rather than invest
in storytelling,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.840
we've spent billions of
dollars on think tanks

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.390
over the past few decades,
on public policy research.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.930
Those PhDs and their work
serve a vital role, no doubt,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.950
but do we believe
white papers and op

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.000
eds can change the country,
that facts, debate,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.540
and our superior
arguments alone can change

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.330
the narrative of a country?

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:07.950
When we do spend
money on media, we

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:12.220
pour billions into political
ads every election cycle.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.600
What do we have to show for it?

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:16.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:17.775
Like I said, what do we--

00:06:17.775 --> 00:06:18.650
it's actually tragic.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.590
Because if you think
about this, the people who

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:23.750
are getting these
advertising dollars,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.390
Comcast, Disney,
are the very people

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:30.350
who use our advertising dollars
to funnel Jimmy Kimmel Show

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.110
and pay for all the
entertainment that attacks us.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:37.790
We're actually funding
our enemy's platforms.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.850
No disrespect, but asking
PhDs and politicians

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.540
to change the culture is
like asking an NFL linebacker

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:47.341
like Clay Matthews
to be a ballerina.

00:06:47.341 --> 00:06:49.305
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:53.720
Rather than do what we naturally
do in any other business, which

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.960
is compete, we have chosen
an interesting alternative

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.775
and an unattractive
one, whining.

00:07:00.775 --> 00:07:03.350
And by the way,
we whine endlessly

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.630
about Hollywood media bias.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.150
And to be fair, we have
a lot to whine about.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:09.920
When's the last time
you saw a businessman

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.100
cast as the good guy and a
bureaucrat as the bad guy

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.390
on a TV show, or a good guy
priest and a bad guy union

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:18.980
boss in a movie?

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.810
A long time ago, actually.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:24.770
It was Rod Steiger playing
the corrupt union boss

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:29.150
and Karl Malden the heroic
priest in Elia Kazan's 1954

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:31.413
masterpiece On the Waterfront.

00:07:31.413 --> 00:07:33.080
Remember the scene
where the dock worker

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.780
is killed by union
goons for testifying

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:37.190
against their bosses?

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:41.390
And there's Father Barry in that
boat calling out the bad guys.

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:45.230
One thug threw a can
at the Father's head.

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.770
Another yelled, "Go back
to your church, Father."

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:51.730
He yells back,
"This is my church."

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:54.410
It made our hair stand up
on our heads, our bodies.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.110
It was so beautiful.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:59.920
So why do stories move people in
ways facts and arguments can't?

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:01.000
It's simple.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.480
Human beings are hard wired
to absorb the narrative form.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.210
Stories have structure,
characters, plot, themes,

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:10.570
and emotion, all of
which reflect life.

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:11.710
Stories change hearts.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:13.300
They change minds.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.140
They change everything.

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.320
That's why it is
easy for me to assert

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:23.390
that storytelling is the world's
oldest and best killer app.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.560
And stories live in the
hearts and minds of people

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.280
long after they're told.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.610
Take Arthur Miller's
plays, which revolve mostly

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.400
around father-son
relationships, but always

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.880
there's that scene that
hammers capitalism,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.090
like the one in Death of a
Salesman where Willy Loman begs

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:43.860
for his job while his boss plays
with a machine on his desk.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.290
Miller wrote that play in 1949.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.420
He died in 2006.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:51.870
People are still
moved by that story.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.880
The power that media
ownership confers

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.700
to the left on the
storytelling front is profound.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.800
And the biggest
advantage is this--

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.230
they choose the
stories that get told.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:02.820
But much more
important, they choose

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.230
the stories that don't get told.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:08.260
That curation power
affects outcomes.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:10.510
If artists want
their stories chosen,

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:14.110
they'd best comport with the
curators' worldview, which

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:17.830
drives artists to censor
themselves without even knowing

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:20.840
it because they need the work.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.170
That's why so many
artists are liberal.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.290
Their bosses are.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:29.040
Another power of media ownership
is its asymmetric nature.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.500
A handful of owners
make the majority of us

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.940
feel as if we're minorities
in our own country.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:38.640
It's why dictators
always seize the media,

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:42.240
because what they're seizing
is their nation's story.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.750
Sadly, the left just did
that, but not by force.

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.340
We just kind of let them.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:51.830
Back in 2004, the
author Louisa Lim

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.260
visited four Beijing
universities.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.990
What would happen, he wondered,
when he showed students

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:59.240
a photo of tank man?

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:00.695
Would they recognize it?

00:10:00.695 --> 00:10:04.490
You know the iconic picture,
the lone man, two shopping bags

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:07.370
in hand, standing in front of
a line of tanks in defiance

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.440
of the crackdown by
the Chinese government

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.050
at Tiananmen Square in 1989.

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:15.050
It turned out that only
15% of the Beijing students

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.120
recognized the picture.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.980
That wasn't an accident.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.850
As Lim explained in his book The
People's Republic of Amnesia--

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.280
Tiananmen Revisited,
the Chinese authorities

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:29.210
were determined to blot out
public memory of the protests.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.280
George Orwell knew why.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:32.578
He said this--

00:10:32.578 --> 00:10:34.370
"The most effective
way to destroy a people

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.680
is to obliterate
their understanding

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:38.630
of their own history."

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:41.000
In America, we have
a different problem.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:45.180
Many on the left who don't
care for America, capitalism,

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.180
or the Constitution
work hard to promote

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:50.210
the most shameful
aspects of our story

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:55.070
while erasing from public memory
our most positive aspects.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:58.970
It's China in reverse, and a
very different kind of amnesia

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:00.590
is setting in.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:03.020
We Conservatives own a
few media platforms--

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:05.870
Fox News, talk
radio, some websites.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:08.120
But it's mostly
news and opinion.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.970
It's programming designed
to feed the choir

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.660
and to preach to the
choir, not grow it.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:17.090
We've invested little in
storytelling platforms

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.760
that will attract new fans
to this great brand called

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:20.950
America.

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:22.890
And we're the business people?

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:24.870
We're the smart guys?

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.240
In spoken word formats,
the storytelling elephant

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.880
in the room is NPR.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.640
They have 30 million people
listening and $350 million

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.910
in annual revenue with only
10% coming from the government.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:38.100
NPR also dominates
the podcast world,

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.610
with five of the top 10
podcasts in the country.

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:44.220
Several years ago, with the
help of some national donors

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.890
and Hillsdale College,
a team of storytellers

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.170
got together to launch
a non-profit alternative

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:53.940
to NPR and a daily two-hour
syndicated storytelling show,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.520
one that celebrates America
and the events and people who

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:58.060
made the country great.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:00.060
From the arts to sports
and business to history,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:01.710
Our American
Stories was designed

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:05.460
to tell the story of
America to Americans.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.050
Many of the stories
revolve around

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:10.830
character, resilience, and
the power and persistence

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:12.040
of imagination.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:13.590
This is a story
about an improbable

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:16.880
story about an author
of America's songbook.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:23.270
Few people write as many hits.

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:26.330
Fewer still write
as many standards.

00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:28.010
Fewer still write
songs that become

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:30.160
part of our national identity.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:31.910
"White Christmas" and
"God Bless America,"

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.250
both of which this
one man wrote,

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:36.590
are a part of our cultural DNA.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.270
He did it without
formal training.

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:41.450
He couldn't read or write music
and played almost entirely

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:43.940
in the key of F-sharp
because it was easier

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:45.500
for his untrained
fingers to play

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:48.530
the elevated,
well-spaced black keys.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.680
He said this-- "The black keys
are right under your fingers.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:55.460
The key of C is for
people who study music."

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:59.260
Irving Berlin's story
is about many things--

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:02.480
hard work, adversity,
creativity,

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.670
about America itself.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:07.040
He was born Israel
Beilin in Russia

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.580
in 1888, one of eight
children and son

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:12.920
to a cantor in the
local synagogue.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.040
His family fled Russia after
their village was destroyed

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:18.170
in an anti-Semitic pogrom.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.750
Berlin's only memory was
of his father, quote,

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:23.300
"lying in a blanket
by the side of a road,

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:26.000
watching his house
burn to the ground."

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.200
His family settled in New York.

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:31.610
But there would be more tragedy
to come in young Irving's life.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:35.330
None was worse than the loss of
his father when he was eight.

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:38.840
It was at a job waiting tables
to support his family that he

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.060
discovered a particular talent.

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:45.140
His tips skyrocketed when
he hummed songs of the day.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:49.310
Singing at tables soon turned
into a lifetime of songwriting.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:51.740
In 1911, his "Alexander's
Ragtime Band"

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:54.290
climbed to number two
on the billboard charts.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:57.180
But ragtime wasn't his thing.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.640
He wanted to create
his own version

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.690
of American music,
something romantic that

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:04.830
appealed to the masses.

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:06.580
He did just that.

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:08.760
He wrote over 1,500 songs.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.250
He told his biographer he
didn't believe in inspiration

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:13.230
and that his most
successful compositions

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:16.230
were the result of hard work.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:18.690
One of his successes
started as a failure.

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:22.260
Berlin wrote "God Bless
America" originally in 1918

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.680
while serving in the army,
but he couldn't find a buyer.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:28.590
In 1938, as Hitler
was rising to power,

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.380
Berlin pulled that
song out of his drawer,

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:35.730
Kate Smith recorded it,
and the rest was history.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:37.530
Berlin's daughter
wrote that her father

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.540
meant every word of that song.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.840
Quote, "It was
the land he loved.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.750
It was his home sweet home.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.780
He, the immigrant who
made good, was saying

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:51.180
thank you to his country."

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.730
Writing one anthem would
be enough for any mortal,

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:58.270
but in 1941, Berlin wrote
another, "White Christmas,"

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:01.410
which would sell 50 million
copies for Bing Crosby

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.130
and become the best-selling
single of all time

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:07.500
and one of the world's most
beloved Christmas songs.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:09.870
Those two anthems may be
the most compelling aspect

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:10.920
of Berlin's legacy.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:13.020
A Jew wrote "White Christmas."

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.900
And a Russian wrote
"God Bless America."

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:17.610
And he was the same damn guy.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.720
Only in America is such
a thing possible, folks.

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:27.690
Only in America is Irving
Berlin's life possible.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:30.990
He died at the age
of 101 a wealthy man,

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:35.430
in large part because he owned
his own publishing catalog.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:37.530
America's artists,
Berlin included,

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:40.980
owe a debt of gratitude
to our nation's founders,

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.290
who secured not just
property rights,

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:46.410
but intellectual property,
too, and the freedom

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:49.710
to write about anything
without recourse

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:51.370
from their own government.

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:53.370
It is why American artists
are the most prolific

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:57.240
in the world, because they
are the freest in the world

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:00.000
and live in a place where
their ideas are protected, too,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.660
and by our nation's Founder
and the Constitution itself,

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:05.130
Article I.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:07.110
The next story is
about that Founder,

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:10.648
because it wasn't any Founder
who wanted to protect ideas.

00:16:10.648 --> 00:16:12.690
He was a Founder who worked
hard to protect ideas

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.510
through something called a
patent because he was himself

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.630
an inventor, a writer,
and a publisher.

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:21.780
Benjamin Franklin understood
the power of ideas.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.730
More importantly, he
understood the value of ideas.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:28.560
He became a very rich man
from Poor Richard's Almanac.

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:31.260
The story reflects
a deep division--

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:32.730
the one I'm about to tell you--

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.210
within our
not-yet-formed country,

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:37.600
a division that split not
just fellow countrymen,

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:39.060
but families, too.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:41.280
And the consequences
for choosing a side

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:46.500
in this division was not simply
a tweet storm or a Facebook

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:47.990
post.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.580
Quote, "An awful silence
pervaded the house

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.240
as we were called
up one after another

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:57.090
to the table of the president of
Congress," Benjamin Rush said,

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:59.140
"to sign what was believed
by many at the time

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:01.330
to be our own death warrant."

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:02.258
Rush was right.

00:17:02.258 --> 00:17:04.300
The British government
considered the Declaration

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:06.550
of Independence a
treasonous document,

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:08.740
and treason was a capital crime.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.510
How divided was America?

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:13.547
One look at Benjamin Franklin's
family tells the story.

00:17:13.547 --> 00:17:15.339
The printer, inventor,
writer, and diplomat

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:18.069
was a driving force of the
American Enlightenment,

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:22.300
but his own home was torn
apart by our first civil war.

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:25.150
Franklin's son William
was illegitimate,

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.460
but he had all the
advantages that any other boy

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.710
in this world could want.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:33.430
Throughout their lives, father
and son were extremely close.

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:35.560
Franklin even used his
influence to secure

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.530
his son the royal
governorship of New Jersey.

00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:40.120
Neither man could
have imagined they'd

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.870
one day be forced to choose
between king and country.

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.600
The father joined the
cause of the Patriots late

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:48.700
and urged his son to join him.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:52.120
William insisted that
dad join the Loyalists.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:56.350
That choice led to the son's
arrest and a two-year stint

00:17:56.350 --> 00:17:58.960
in the worst prison in
America, the Litchfield

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:01.390
Gaol in Connecticut.

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:03.640
Quote, "It was a
destination of last resort

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.740
for outlaws condemned
to be hanged,

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:09.280
convicted murderers,
reprobates, and traitors,"

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:12.040
wrote historian David Epstein.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:15.520
After his release from prison,
William emigrated to England,

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:17.740
never to return home.

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:20.470
A few years before his
death, Ben Franklin

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:22.780
received a letter from
his son, who was hoping

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:25.150
for a chance at reconciliation.

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:27.700
The father wrote back
promptly-- and imagine

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:30.670
getting this kind of a
letter from your dad-- quote,

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:33.070
"Nothing has hurt
me so much, son,

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:35.290
and affected me with
such keen sensation

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:39.640
as to find myself deserted
in my old age by my only son,

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:42.130
and not only deserted,
but to find him taking up

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:45.560
arms against me in a cause
wherein my good fame, fortune,

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:48.730
and life were all at stake."

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:50.080
That was it.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:52.030
The two men would
never reconcile.

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.230
Franklin died a wealthy man.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:57.040
He left his son nothing.

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:58.810
The political divide
today is still

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:01.990
about power in a distant
city, but that distant city

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:03.220
isn't London.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:04.960
It's Washington, DC.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:07.450
It's a fight we've been having
since our founding, fights

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:10.330
about power, fights
about sovereignty.

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:13.600
That last story was situated
in the 18th century.

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:16.300
This next is about one of
America's great 20th-century

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:18.820
leaders, Martin Luther King.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:22.750
His Christian faith was an
animating force in his life,

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:27.580
as was the book he dedicated
his life's study to, the Bible.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:30.580
Bill Maher, the comedian,
once described the Bible

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:33.550
as a book most Christians
don't actually read.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:34.660
He said this--

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:36.340
"It's like a software license.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:40.220
Christians scroll down to the
bottom and click I Agree."

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:41.960
Martin Luther King
thought differently.

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:44.190
The Bible wasn't a
software license.

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:47.750
It was software code,
a deep mysterious code

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:51.920
authored by a God for
man's eternal purposes.

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:55.220
Indeed, he found the Bible so
compelling, King, so relevant

00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:59.600
in the 20th century, that his
undergraduate degree and PhD

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:02.120
were both in Bible studies.

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:04.910
Leaving God out of Martin
Luther King's life story,

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:07.730
and the Bible, is like leaving
automobiles out of Henry

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:11.630
Ford's life or hamburgers
out of Ray Kroc's.

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:13.190
But that's precisely
what the media

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:18.460
does at every turn every year
when they celebrate his life.

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:20.250
Take the last night
of King's life.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:24.300
As he headed into Memphis in
1968, he knew he was in danger.

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:26.430
Quote, "There's no way
you can keep somebody

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.950
from killing you,"
he told reporters,

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:31.210
"if they really
want to kill you."

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:34.020
Bomb threats delayed his
flight into Memphis that night.

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.370
And though King was troubled,
he told supporters, quote,

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:40.010
"I'd rather be
dead than afraid."

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:41.840
Then came his speech
at the Mason Temple

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:43.490
the night of April 3.

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:48.710
It was as if the 39-year-old
knew his life would soon end.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:51.140
A major theme of
that speech was fear.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:55.570
And by the way, is it not
a major theme of the Bible?

00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:57.955
The other was the story
"the Good Samaritan,"

00:20:57.955 --> 00:21:01.030
a parable told by Jesus
in the Gospel of Luke.

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:04.300
King told the audience about a
trip he and his wife made along

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:08.230
the Jericho Road and why
he thought two men passed

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:11.640
a stranger in need
and why one didn't.

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:14.350
Quote, and this is from
the sermon directly,

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:16.810
"It's a winding,
meandering, dangerous road,

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:19.600
conducive to ambushing,"
he told the audience.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.420
"And you know, it's possible
that the priest and the Levite

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:24.310
looked over at that
man on the ground

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:27.500
and wondered if the
robbers were still around.

00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:29.500
Or it's possible they
felt the man on the ground

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:32.470
was acting like he'd been robbed
and hurt in order to lure them

00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:34.840
there for easy seizure.

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:36.730
And so the first
question the Levite

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:39.700
asked was, as I stop
to help this man, what

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:41.500
will happen to me?

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:44.920
Then the Good Samaritan came by,
and he reversed the question.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:49.070
If I don't stop to help this
man, what will happen to him?

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:52.487
That's the question before
you tonight," King preached.

00:21:52.487 --> 00:21:54.070
A few minutes later,
the speech, which

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:58.920
had by now turned into a sermon,
started its prophetic turn.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:02.250
"Like anybody," he said, "I'd
like to live a long life.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.290
Longevity has its place.

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:06.820
But I'm not concerned
about that now.

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:09.120
I just want to do
God's will," King

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:11.055
told the spellbound audience.

00:22:11.055 --> 00:22:12.930
"He's allowed me to go
up to the mountaintop,

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:14.490
and I've looked over it.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:16.470
And I've seen the promised land.

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:18.520
And I may not get
there with you,

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:21.240
but I want you to know tonight
that as we a people will,

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:25.230
in the end, finally, we will
get to the promised land."

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:28.620
On the eve of April 4, not
long before he was gunned down

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:31.170
at the Lorraine
Hotel in Memphis,

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:33.480
King's last words
were to a musician

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:36.140
named Ben Branch,
who was scheduled

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:38.510
to perform at an event
later on that night.

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:41.450
"Ben," he said, "make sure
you play 'Take My Hand,

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:43.330
Precious Lord.'

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:44.940
Play it real slow.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.890
Play it pretty."

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.600
The enduring truth
of this story,

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:51.180
one the media cannot
rewrite, is this--

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:54.720
Martin Luther King's desire
to serve the God he loved

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:58.560
changed forever the
country he loved.

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:00.810
This last story that
I'm about to tell you

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:04.170
is the story of a
city and a disaster,

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:08.670
the Great Chicago Fire of 1871,
and the even more remarkable

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:12.220
story of the great
Chicago recovery.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:15.250
Experts disagree on
how that fire started.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.020
Some say it was a cow.

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:18.820
Others say it was vandals.

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:22.090
But everyone agrees on
why the fire started.

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:24.730
It was Chicago's
remarkable growth.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:28.480
In 1840, the city had
a mere 4,500 residents.

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:31.780
10 years later, it
passed the 30,000 mark.

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:38.800
By 1860, it had grown to
100,000, and by 1870, 300,000.

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:40.780
What led to all that growth?

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:44.328
As the saying goes,
location, location, location.

00:23:44.328 --> 00:23:46.120
Chicago was near the
center of the country,

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:48.280
near where the waterways
and railways met.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:51.010
It was a perfect place
for anything and anyone

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:53.020
to get anywhere.

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:54.630
Timing also played a part.

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:56.380
America was going
through a transformation

00:23:56.380 --> 00:23:59.560
from a rural power to
an industrial power.

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:02.830
But how did all of that
growth, all of that life,

00:24:02.830 --> 00:24:05.830
lead to a near-death experience?

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:07.930
As a Chicago
historian explained,

00:24:07.930 --> 00:24:11.630
Chicago had to build quickly, so
they built it all out of wood.

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:14.170
It was quick and easy and cheap.

00:24:14.170 --> 00:24:16.250
Mother nature played
her part, too.

00:24:16.250 --> 00:24:19.390
Meteorologist Tom Skilling said,
quote, "It was an exceedingly

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:20.980
dry summer and fall.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:22.480
Worse still, winter
was approaching,

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:26.270
and those wood houses were
filled with firewood."

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:29.380
The Chicago Fire, it turns
out, wasn't just predictable.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:31.310
It was inevitable.

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:33.610
It started out on the west
side and quickly roared out

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:35.920
of control, a steady
wind propelling

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:40.210
the inferno from block to block
and even across the Chicago

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:41.500
River.

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:44.800
30 hours later, the fire
finally ran out of fuel

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:47.230
because there was
nothing left to burn.

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:50.740
The fire claimed nearly 300
lives, a third of the city

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:55.090
lay in ruins, and 100,000
people were homeless.

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:59.410
Most of the hotels, stores,
and banks were destroyed.

00:24:59.410 --> 00:25:01.130
By the way, that's State Street.

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:03.760
A picture in a second will
show you that same shot,

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:06.700
same angle, 20 years later.

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:08.470
What happened next
revealed the character

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:11.230
of the city and the nation.

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:16.120
There was no FEMA back then, no
Illinois Emergency Management,

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:20.410
no one to blame, no one to sue,
and no appetite for victimhood.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:22.510
Even before the bricks
stopped smoking,

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:25.360
the city's leaders were
planning a comeback.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:29.560
The Reverend Robert Collier told
his congregation these words.

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:31.930
"We have not lost our geography.

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:35.100
Nature called the lakes, the
forests, and the prairies

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:39.040
together in convention
long before we were born.

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:41.230
And they have decided
that on this spot

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:44.440
a great city would be rebuilt."

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:47.590
Within 48 hours, 12 of the
29 banks that burned down

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:49.750
were operating in
makeshift facilities

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:51.550
to get money to customers.

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:54.040
Bankers got busy
raising capital.

00:25:54.040 --> 00:25:56.860
One sent letters to
bankers all over the globe

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.440
urging them to
invest and loan money

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:01.840
to the great people of Chicago.

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:04.120
Chicago was rebuilt at
an unimaginable pace.

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:07.830
By 1880, its population
reached 500,000.

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:10.210
And by 1890, it
passed the 1 million

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:14.380
mark, becoming the second
biggest city in America.

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:15.940
Same street.

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:18.240
Same camera angle.

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:20.100
The people of
Chicago did all of it

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:23.620
without the help of the
state or federal government.

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:27.420
American optimism, resilience,
character, and capitalism

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:30.650
itself rebuilt the city.

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:34.250
Folks, we must start telling the
story of America to Americans,

00:26:34.250 --> 00:26:37.040
stories about all that's
good and decent and beautiful

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:38.640
about this country.

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:41.230
We must address matters
of the heart, too.

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:43.400
Not talking about
love is where we all

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:47.230
go wrong, because it's
love that builds a city.

00:26:47.230 --> 00:26:49.240
We must tell stories
about faith, too,

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.940
and the beautiful things
we do because of our faith.

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:55.640
America is unimaginable
without it,

00:26:55.640 --> 00:26:57.110
because the battle
lines in America

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:59.480
are not just between
left and right.

00:26:59.480 --> 00:27:03.780
In the end, they're
between up versus down.

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:07.710
And that is the material world
versus the spiritual world.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:09.680
Folks, to continue
to let the left alone

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:13.400
tell the story of America
isn't just gross negligence,

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:15.380
it's cultural suicide.

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:17.518
We have great stories to tell.

00:27:17.518 --> 00:27:18.560
Let's start telling them.

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:19.520
How hard can it be?

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:22.320
After all, liberals do it.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:23.413
That's all, folks.

00:27:23.413 --> 00:27:26.864
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:37.608 --> 00:27:39.400
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for some questions,

00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:41.660
and there are microphones.

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:43.540
Lee, do you want to [INAUDIBLE]?

00:27:43.540 --> 00:27:45.420
LEE HABEEB: Sure.

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:47.310
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:49.260
It's a wonderful thing
that you're doing.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:49.720
LEE HABEEB: I appreciate it.

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:50.310
Thank you.

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:52.210
AUDIENCE: But let me
tell you, as a writer,

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:54.420
what the difficulties are.

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:58.830
Editors, publishers, are looking
for a certain point of view.

00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:01.020
And it's hard to even get
through the front door.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:03.390
And I was a nationally
syndicated columnist

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:05.940
in 122 papers.

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:07.740
The generation of
today-- course,

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:09.760
I was a different generation--

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:13.830
doesn't want to talk to me
because of my point of view.

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:19.810
The grants that you try to go
for as far as the internships,

00:28:19.810 --> 00:28:22.830
the money that even
the government gives,

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:25.320
has a different point of view.

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:29.610
And the newspapers
are owned, the media--

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:31.870
why aren't we buying?

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:33.120
Why aren't we buying them?

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:35.640
Why aren't we spending
money in order

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:41.510
to create our own market as far
as storytelling is concerned?

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:43.078
So those are just
some of the things

00:28:43.078 --> 00:28:45.620
that I would like you to address
if you have any ideas on it.

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:46.787
LEE HABEEB: I'd be happy to.

00:28:46.787 --> 00:28:48.893
And I think you're--

00:28:48.893 --> 00:28:49.810
Laura Ingraham and I--

00:28:49.810 --> 00:28:50.880
I went to law school
with Laura Ingraham

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:52.650
and started her national show.

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:56.440
In 1996, we got hired by
MSNBC, believe it or not,

00:28:56.440 --> 00:28:59.938
to do a TV show on MSNBC, back
when nobody was watching it

00:28:59.938 --> 00:29:01.480
and they pretended
not to be liberal.

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:03.960
Only I was stuck in
Secaucus, New Jersey all day,

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:06.000
and it was miserable, and
everybody was liberal.

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:07.250
But there was this
show in the morning

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:08.650
called The [INAUDIBLE] Show.

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:10.400
And Laura and I learned
a lot about radio,

00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:11.460
and we got into radio.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:15.010
But in 1996, [? 15-year ?]
[? hurt ?] had [? started. ?]

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:18.360
CNN [? had it. ?] A guy named
Roger Ailes got together with

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:20.270
a billionaire named
Rupert Murdoch,

00:29:20.270 --> 00:29:23.280
and they decided to slide a
lot of money up to the table

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:25.490
and say, let's compete.

00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:26.910
And they competed.

00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:29.220
And Fox News won.

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:31.290
And I've seen it
happen again and again.

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:35.250
Let's take a film director that
I think we all know and love,

00:29:35.250 --> 00:29:36.180
Clint Eastwood.

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:39.810
Since 1991, Clint Eastwood
has done 22 or 23 movies,

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:41.280
I'm not sure of which.

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:43.640
Eight have been number
one or number two

00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:45.900
and over a billion
dollars in the box office.

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:48.450
He has total creative
control of his movies.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:50.070
And well, why not?

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:51.420
They make money.

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:52.920
I think we're getting
to a place now

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:54.570
where a lot of our
donors and investors

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:56.680
who've spent a lot of
money on political ads

00:29:56.680 --> 00:29:58.160
and a lot of money
in other places

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:01.220
are now starting to think
hard about storytelling.

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:03.740
And the good news is
that the barrier to entry

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:05.527
are much lower than
they used to be,

00:30:05.527 --> 00:30:06.860
much lower than they used to be.

00:30:06.860 --> 00:30:09.090
And there's just
tremendous demand, I mean,

00:30:09.090 --> 00:30:11.750
that every Clint Eastwood movie
is number one or number two.

00:30:11.750 --> 00:30:13.130
Think about The Unforgiven.

00:30:13.130 --> 00:30:14.560
Think about Gran Torino.

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.760
I mean, there was a movie
with a Catholic priest who

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:19.130
was trying to do good,
trying to influence

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.222
the old cranky bastard, right?

00:30:21.222 --> 00:30:23.180
And you would have thought
he was incorrigible,

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:24.400
except what does he do?

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:26.030
These total strangers
who he didn't

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:29.720
like, who he insulted routinely,
he ended up loving, willing

00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:32.470
his perfect,
perfect car to them,

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:35.330
and in an act of
self-sacrifice gets

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:40.100
himself shot to free that
family from thugs and gangsters.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:41.720
It was a beautiful movie.

00:30:41.720 --> 00:30:43.430
And Americans were
craving for it.

00:30:43.430 --> 00:30:46.618
And our capitalists just have to
start stepping up to the plate

00:30:46.618 --> 00:30:47.660
and putting the money up.

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:49.202
And that's why we're
doing what we're

00:30:49.202 --> 00:30:52.580
doing, because one success
can beget more successes.

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.390
Moreover, and I'll
leave you with this,

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:56.900
one of the things that's
missing is executive talent.

00:30:56.900 --> 00:30:59.270
There are two publicly
traded companies in America

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:02.840
that are run by conservatives,
Fox News and Salem Media Group.

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:05.480
And I'm the VP of content
at Salem Media Group.

00:31:05.480 --> 00:31:07.530
If and when I decide
to leave that position,

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:10.670
a very nice paying position,
that position will be filled.

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:12.770
And guess how many other
jobs are out there for me

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:13.920
immediately?

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:14.780
None.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:16.280
I leave this thing
and I'm a liberal

00:31:16.280 --> 00:31:19.020
and I got to start my own
thing, there's 25 other jobs

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:21.200
if I decide to drop back in.

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:24.110
So I think these are things
we know we have to address,

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:25.940
and we're about to
start addressing them.

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:29.160
I got so tired of complaining
and whining about it

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:31.550
that I went ahead and
talked to some capitalists

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:34.720
and they liked the idea, gave us
some money, and said, go at it.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:38.270
So I think this is more about
us not complaining anymore, not

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:39.900
whining about our circumstance.

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:44.660
I mean, goodness, Washington
looked out into Brooklyn Harbor

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:48.200
and saw an armada coming in--

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:52.040
slightly more difficult
problem, right?

00:31:52.040 --> 00:31:53.770
So I just think that--

00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:56.450
you know, the word Stockholm
syndrome always interested me,

00:31:56.450 --> 00:31:59.390
because, you know, my wife
had been sexually abused

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:00.470
for six years.

00:32:00.470 --> 00:32:02.090
And I said to her, well, how--

00:32:02.090 --> 00:32:03.920
why didn't you leave?

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:05.600
And she said, you just don't.

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:07.300
You start to identify
with the captor.

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:11.060
The captor gets in your head,
and pretty soon you're stuck.

00:32:11.060 --> 00:32:12.530
And the real power
of the media is

00:32:12.530 --> 00:32:15.770
that we're all in a sort of
Stockholm syndrome-like effect.

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:17.380
We keep seeing the
media coming at us,

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:18.710
and we think it's hopeless.

00:32:18.710 --> 00:32:20.360
Meanwhile, we keep
winning elections.

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:23.740
We heard that Barack Obama had
Google, and he had Facebook,

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:27.050
and that the ascendancy of
the digital social revolution

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:28.820
would lead to a
Democratic hegemony,

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:31.810
and the Tea Party came
in and swept the day.

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:33.900
[INAUDIBLE] had nobody
rooting for him.

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:36.453
And there was just
NBC, ABC, and CBS.

00:32:36.453 --> 00:32:37.870
They were all
rooting against him.

00:32:37.870 --> 00:32:38.970
There was no Fox News.

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:39.840
There was no Drudge.

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:40.920
There was nothing.

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:43.870
He won 49 states
in his second race.

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:47.250
So the American people
just need to be fed.

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:49.350
And I think we're trying
to prove that authority

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:50.080
with our proposition.

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:51.050
But I know the problem.

00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:52.290
Look, I experienced
it a good deal

00:32:52.290 --> 00:32:54.250
of my life in the 1990s
when Laura and I were

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:55.320
trying to start.

00:32:55.320 --> 00:32:57.240
There was no talk radio.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:33:00.510
I mean, there was maybe one talk
station in every fifth market.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:03.600
And it wasn't until 2001 when
we got nationally syndicated,

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:05.820
Hannity got syndicated.
[? Matt ?] got syndicated.

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:07.798
And suddenly, there
was a lot of content.

00:33:07.798 --> 00:33:09.840
And so suddenly there were
a lot of talk stations

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:10.965
to [? park ?] that content.

00:33:10.965 --> 00:33:14.760
And by the way, you want to talk
about a conservative dialogue--

00:33:14.760 --> 00:33:17.160
there's 45 million people
listening to conservative talk

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:17.990
radio.

00:33:17.990 --> 00:33:20.380
And it has made all the
difference in the world.

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:22.860
We have come to dominate
talk radio in a way

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:25.590
that no liberal, no leftist
could have imagined.

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:28.710
I remember, and I'm sure all
you do, the first time you heard

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:30.060
that cry of Rush Limbaugh.

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:32.340
You went, that's me.

00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:36.430
Finally, finally someone's
talking to me in my language.

00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:39.210
So if we make movies that
people want, they'll come.

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:41.210
I have no question if we
build it, they'll come.

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:41.793
Next question.

00:33:45.692 --> 00:33:47.648
AUDIENCE: What's your
greatest source of hope?

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:53.230
LEE HABEEB: God.

00:33:53.230 --> 00:33:55.680
You know, Malcolm Gladwell
was on Laura's show.

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:57.450
And she's always asking
me for questions,

00:33:57.450 --> 00:33:59.740
because we love to get
the authors comfortable,

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:01.470
and then we ask them a zinger.

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:04.090
And well, we had a zinger for
Malcolm, because, of course,

00:34:04.090 --> 00:34:07.500
when he was writing his book
about David and Goliath,

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:09.270
he told a really great story.

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:11.820
And he talked about
how David had prepared

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:14.250
for years with those
five smooth stones

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:16.860
and how Goliath was his
stumbling adversary,

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:18.570
and that David had
all the advantages

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:21.989
and Goliath didn't, because the
new game was about speed, not

00:34:21.989 --> 00:34:23.040
about size.

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:24.840
You think David
knew any of that?

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:27.719
You think David got there and
won that battle just because

00:34:27.719 --> 00:34:29.370
of all that preparation?

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:31.570
So she let Malcolm
opine and opine,

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:35.170
but Malcolm had left one
thing out of his book.

00:34:35.170 --> 00:34:38.580
David had God on his side.

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:40.080
And he knew it.

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:41.010
This helps.

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:42.495
So God gives me hope.

00:34:42.495 --> 00:34:44.370
And going on campuses
actually gives me hope.

00:34:44.370 --> 00:34:45.840
I live in Oxford, Mississippi.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:47.429
That's where we do our show.

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:52.110
And I see young people battling
back in their own ways,

00:34:52.110 --> 00:34:54.139
often just through--

00:34:54.139 --> 00:34:57.150
just a one of these to
the campus protesters.

00:34:57.150 --> 00:34:59.730
And I think sometimes we
give the campus protesters

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:02.040
a little too much credit.

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:04.008
I don't like what's
happening in our campuses.

00:35:04.008 --> 00:35:05.300
But our young people are smart.

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:06.360
I found Ronald Reagan.

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:06.860
I got you.

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:08.040
That was my first vote.

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:10.860
Everybody told me, oh, young
people won't vote for Regan.

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:11.860
They won't vote for him.

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:12.570
He's old.

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:14.028
They won't-- but
what were we told?

00:35:14.028 --> 00:35:16.410
He was going to push
[INAUDIBLE] off the cliff.

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:18.160
He was going to get
us into a nuclear war.

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:19.390
He was an actor.

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:20.310
He was an idiot.

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:21.430
He was lazy.

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:23.797
I mean, the prevailing
theme about Reagan

00:35:23.797 --> 00:35:26.130
was that he was going to lose
and that, you know, nobody

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:27.213
was going to vote for him.

00:35:27.213 --> 00:35:28.338
Meanwhile, I voted for him.

00:35:28.338 --> 00:35:29.546
Laura Ingraham voted for him.

00:35:29.546 --> 00:35:32.040
[INAUDIBLE]---- every young
person I know voted for him,

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:34.200
because Jimmy Carter was
saying the following.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:36.600
America's best
days are behind us.

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:38.400
Put on a sweater and
love your thermostat.

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:40.790
And I thought, that sucks.

00:35:40.790 --> 00:35:41.820
That sucks.

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:44.420
I'm going with the old guy who
says our best days are ahead.

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:46.710
And by the way, let me tell
you about the old days,

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:48.400
because we're a great country.

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:49.530
We're a great people.

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:52.230
And so I think half
of our problem is us.

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:54.480
We walk around
acting as if we're

00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:56.220
losing when we're winning.

00:35:56.220 --> 00:35:59.050
And we act like we have no hope.

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:01.300
And this is what the left
does and their media control

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:02.178
does best of all.

00:36:02.178 --> 00:36:03.803
Look, if you believe
that there's a God

00:36:03.803 --> 00:36:06.780
and there's a Satan, Satan
wants to steal your joy

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:07.860
and make you angry.

00:36:07.860 --> 00:36:11.143
So when we're angry, we're
working on behalf of the left.

00:36:11.143 --> 00:36:12.810
When we're loving and
we're reaching out

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:14.268
and we're telling
stories and we're

00:36:14.268 --> 00:36:16.740
reaching out to our
neighbors, there's a delight.

00:36:16.740 --> 00:36:18.570
Once a year-- I'll tell you
a quick story-- once a year,

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:21.153
there's this Lebanese professor
at Ole Miss who invites me in,

00:36:21.153 --> 00:36:22.860
so I get to speak to 500 kids.

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:24.023
The faculty goes crazy.

00:36:24.023 --> 00:36:24.690
He doesn't care.

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:25.380
He's tenured.

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:26.550
He's a Christian and
he's a conservative.

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:27.883
He goes, Habeeb, do what you do.

00:36:27.883 --> 00:36:29.580
So I get 500 kids in a room.

00:36:29.580 --> 00:36:31.300
And I see something
very interesting.

00:36:31.300 --> 00:36:33.040
I tell everybody to
close their eyes.

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:35.520
I say, how many of you believe
in the thing called love,

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:38.220
even though it's not provable,
even though no scientists can

00:36:38.220 --> 00:36:38.842
measure it?

00:36:38.842 --> 00:36:40.800
How many of you believe
in a thing called love?

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:42.167
Every single hand goes up.

00:36:42.167 --> 00:36:44.500
And by the way, I don't have
them look, and I filmed it.

00:36:44.500 --> 00:36:46.630
And so now I say,
keep your eyes closed.

00:36:46.630 --> 00:36:50.380
How many of you, if you met
the proper person in your life,

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.560
and it was your soulmate
or someone you really

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:54.030
thought you could spend
the rest of your life with,

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:55.530
how many of you would
want to get married?

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:57.030
Almost all the hands stayed up.

00:36:57.030 --> 00:36:58.130
Be still my heart.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:36:59.262
Almost total agreement.

00:36:59.262 --> 00:37:00.720
All these kids from
different walks

00:37:00.720 --> 00:37:02.750
of life, all different
ideas [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:02.750 --> 00:37:03.810
And then I keep going.

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:05.352
How many of you want
to have children

00:37:05.352 --> 00:37:06.720
if you found that dream person?

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:08.180
Almost all the hands stayed up.

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:10.380
Now, maybe they'd want
to have them later.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:11.720
That's beside the point.

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:15.010
So I keep their heads down
and their eyes closed.

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:17.490
I said, now, how many of
you, if you had two children,

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:20.110
one did their homework,
one did all their work,

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:22.137
one was dutiful, and
then you had another

00:37:22.137 --> 00:37:24.720
who didn't do anything, stayed
in the room, played loud music,

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:26.928
and just played video games
and didn't do any chores,

00:37:26.928 --> 00:37:29.190
how many of you would give
them the same allowance?

00:37:29.190 --> 00:37:30.700
Not a single hand went up.

00:37:30.700 --> 00:37:32.190
So then I said,
raise your heads.

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:33.660
I said, let's play the video.

00:37:33.660 --> 00:37:36.000
Who said we don't agree
on a lot of things?

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:37.470
And then I asked some questions.

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:40.820
Did you create inequality by
giving one kid an allowance

00:37:40.820 --> 00:37:42.810
and one getting nothing?

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:44.917
And is it a bad thing
or a good thing?

00:37:44.917 --> 00:37:46.500
And I asked just a
few more questions,

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:48.060
and [INAUDIBLE]
they had a line of,

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:51.135
like, 300 kids going,
[? did you ?] teach you?

00:37:51.135 --> 00:37:52.510
And I didn't give
them an answer.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:54.050
I just asked them a
bunch of questions.

00:37:54.050 --> 00:37:55.258
And Chris [INAUDIBLE] and I--

00:37:55.258 --> 00:37:58.080
Chris is on the board of
[? APR, ?] was telling me

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.180
a story about watching
Milton Friedman back

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:02.160
when he was at the
University of Michigan,

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:04.560
and watching Milton
Friedman on, of all places,

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:05.970
the Phil Donahue show.

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:06.690
Google it.

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:09.150
Put out Phil Donahue
and Milton Friedman.

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:11.280
All Phil Donahue
is doing is asking

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:14.520
questions in a proper series.

00:38:14.520 --> 00:38:16.730
And Phil Donahue is,
like, gesticulating.

00:38:16.730 --> 00:38:18.660
By the time, five or
six minutes passes,

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:21.202
Phil Donahue doesn't know what
to do with himself, because he

00:38:21.202 --> 00:38:24.450
can't answer a series of
very simple questions.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:26.680
So I have tremendous hope,
not only for the country--

00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:27.900
we've got God on our side.

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:29.525
We've got the
Constitution on our side.

00:38:29.525 --> 00:38:30.750
We've got an amazing people.

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:31.875
And I got to tell you this.

00:38:31.875 --> 00:38:33.530
We have immigration on our side.

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:36.870
We retold one story
about a Chinese-American

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:38.890
who did a thought experiment.

00:38:38.890 --> 00:38:41.490
He was wondering
how many Americans

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:43.770
try to become Chinese citizens.

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:45.270
So he said, what the heck?

00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:47.080
Let me see what
that would be like.

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:48.372
So he goes through the process.

00:38:48.372 --> 00:38:50.038
And when he's going
through the process,

00:38:50.038 --> 00:38:52.100
he discovers that not a
single American that year

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:54.023
had tried to become
a Chinese citizen.

00:38:54.023 --> 00:38:55.440
So then he calls
some pals that he

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:57.908
had because he had gone to
some overseas fancy school,

00:38:57.908 --> 00:39:00.450
and it turns out they were doing
the thought experiment, too.

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:02.070
There wasn't a person
in Europe who'd

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:04.080
tried to become a
Chinese citizen.

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:07.320
And at the end of this little
sort of story that he told us,

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:09.450
he said, you know, I'm
so hopeful about America,

00:39:09.450 --> 00:39:12.480
because we're great at
creating Chinese-Americans.

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:17.490
But China is really bad at
creating American-Chinese.

00:39:17.490 --> 00:39:18.630
And this is true.

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:20.250
And so this is our advantage.

00:39:20.250 --> 00:39:23.050
The world wants to come here.

00:39:23.050 --> 00:39:25.560
And I love that the left
says we're a racist nation

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:28.230
and yet all these
dark-skinned immigrants

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:31.500
are trying to come here, like my
Lebanese and Sicilian parents.

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:34.330
So their claims are false.

00:39:34.330 --> 00:39:35.610
They get us too angry.

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:37.890
If we were a little more
confident in our step,

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:39.390
a little more
confident in our walk,

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:41.890
we'd do what Reagan always did,
because remember what Reagan

00:39:41.890 --> 00:39:43.210
did when they'd do what he did?

00:39:43.210 --> 00:39:44.752
He would just go,
there you go again.

00:39:44.752 --> 00:39:46.470
He laughed it off.

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:48.450
That's how confident
he was in his views.

00:39:48.450 --> 00:39:49.410
He shrugged it off.

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:53.700
And how humiliating for your
opponent to just be laughed at.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:54.740
So very hopeful.

00:39:54.740 --> 00:39:55.470
Always hopeful.

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:56.345
I got God on my side.

00:39:58.760 --> 00:40:00.782
Any other questions?

00:40:00.782 --> 00:40:01.282
Yes.

00:40:04.288 --> 00:40:05.830
AUDIENCE: You
mentioned the old days.

00:40:05.830 --> 00:40:08.020
A lot of things that
we like about America

00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:10.810
were there at the beginning, but
that's not where they started.

00:40:10.810 --> 00:40:12.550
Is there ever a need
to not just tell

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:14.680
America's stories,
but the stories that

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:16.350
made America what it was?

00:40:16.350 --> 00:40:18.850
LEE HABEEB: Yeah, and we do--
what we tend to do on our show

00:40:18.850 --> 00:40:20.330
is a mixture of things.

00:40:20.330 --> 00:40:24.610
So for every story we tell about
George Washington, which we'll

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:29.350
do, we'll tell a story about
some young Venezuelan immigrant

00:40:29.350 --> 00:40:32.690
who left the oil
patch of Venezuela.

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:34.810
This particular gentleman
left when Hugo Chavez

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:36.940
consolidated the oil industry.

00:40:36.940 --> 00:40:39.220
And why he left was
because the moment

00:40:39.220 --> 00:40:41.920
he consolidated the oil
industry, Chavez's pals

00:40:41.920 --> 00:40:45.190
all got the jobs and
the competent people

00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:47.590
all left because, well,
you know what happens

00:40:47.590 --> 00:40:49.290
when nepotism rules the day.

00:40:49.290 --> 00:40:50.280
Incompetence wins.

00:40:50.280 --> 00:40:51.313
Competence is punished.

00:40:51.313 --> 00:40:52.480
So they said, what the heck?

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:53.210
Let's get out of here.

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:54.293
Let's go to the oil patch.

00:40:54.293 --> 00:40:57.130
And with no money to their
name, he and his friends

00:40:57.130 --> 00:41:00.020
worked hard, cleaned
fracking trucks,

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:02.080
bought a couple
fracking trucks, and now

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:04.270
run a multimillion-dollar
business.

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:06.880
And so connecting our
past to our present,

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:07.880
and why did that happen?

00:41:07.880 --> 00:41:09.755
And he started talking
about property rights.

00:41:09.755 --> 00:41:11.140
He started to talk
about capital.

00:41:11.140 --> 00:41:14.230
He started talking about how
a contract meant something.

00:41:14.230 --> 00:41:16.870
Now, these are things we
take for granted [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:18.535
You know, we play this piece--

00:41:18.535 --> 00:41:20.660
I think it's almost worth
playing if I can find it.

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:23.800
We do an hour on Justice
Scalia's life every year.

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:25.180
I mean, shame on us.

00:41:25.180 --> 00:41:29.260
Has the left done nine
documentaries by now on RBG,

00:41:29.260 --> 00:41:29.860
or 10?

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:31.990
I mean, there's a new
one comes out every day.

00:41:31.990 --> 00:41:34.948
I walked out of Ole Miss,
of the one documentary

00:41:34.948 --> 00:41:37.490
that they played there, because
I wanted to see the reaction,

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:38.920
to see what the professors did.

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:40.878
They had about 1,000
people at their law school

00:41:40.878 --> 00:41:42.880
watch a documentary
about Ginsburg.

00:41:42.880 --> 00:41:44.875
And as I walked out,
I'm just talking

00:41:44.875 --> 00:41:46.750
to some of the students,
a lot of undergrads,

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:48.125
a lot of people
in the community,

00:41:48.125 --> 00:41:50.740
and they were just so horrified
when I asked them, hey,

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:52.340
did you know that
Ruth Bader Ginsburg

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:55.590
wasn't the first female
Supreme Court Justice?

00:41:55.590 --> 00:41:57.070
They didn't know that.

00:41:57.070 --> 00:41:59.230
[INAUDIBLE] through
this documentary,

00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:00.665
there was like one
little passage,

00:42:00.665 --> 00:42:03.040
there was like one little
picture of Sandra Day O'Connor,

00:42:03.040 --> 00:42:04.160
and that was it.

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:08.290
And we did a story about
Scalia in his own words.

00:42:08.290 --> 00:42:10.690
And not just about the
Constitution, right?

00:42:10.690 --> 00:42:14.410
I mean, he's spoken about so
many things in so many places.

00:42:14.410 --> 00:42:16.930
And when people
hear Scalia talk,

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:18.430
it's just a remarkable thing.

00:42:18.430 --> 00:42:19.960
The most powerful
thing, on the day

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:21.970
before he died, the
day after he died,

00:42:21.970 --> 00:42:24.760
we asked people for their
favorite Scalia stories.

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:29.830
And this one gentleman who
writes for the Acton Institute

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:31.630
told this story
about how he would

00:42:31.630 --> 00:42:34.630
go to church fairly
regularly on a weekday

00:42:34.630 --> 00:42:36.910
afternoon in the same church
near the Court, Catholic

00:42:36.910 --> 00:42:38.442
Church, that Scalia went to.

00:42:38.442 --> 00:42:40.150
And one day, the church
was pretty empty.

00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:41.317
He was standing in the back.

00:42:41.317 --> 00:42:42.670
Scalia didn't notice him.

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:45.188
He didn't notice
Scalia at first,

00:42:45.188 --> 00:42:46.355
but then he saw the justice.

00:42:46.355 --> 00:42:49.347
And he saw a woman walk up
to the justice who looked,

00:42:49.347 --> 00:42:50.930
as he had said, like
the kind of woman

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:52.960
you'd avoid on
the street-- sores

00:42:52.960 --> 00:42:56.700
on her hands, open
sores, a homeless person,

00:42:56.700 --> 00:42:58.360
a troubled person, he said.

00:42:58.360 --> 00:43:01.150
He said, and I want Justice
Scalia put his arm around her.

00:43:01.150 --> 00:43:04.600
I watched Justice
Scalia stroke her arm.

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:06.960
I watched him touch
her, whisper words

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:10.420
of reassurance and comfort to
her for about five minutes.

00:43:10.420 --> 00:43:12.740
And then whatever he
said did reassure her.

00:43:12.740 --> 00:43:15.040
It looked like he had
given her a few dollars,

00:43:15.040 --> 00:43:17.350
again tapped her on the
back, rubbed her back,

00:43:17.350 --> 00:43:18.590
and sent her on her way.

00:43:18.590 --> 00:43:20.220
And then he went on his way.

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:23.980
And he said, and then I
saw a man, a beautiful man,

00:43:23.980 --> 00:43:27.730
who was honoring his God and
doing it the way God commanded,

00:43:27.730 --> 00:43:29.920
which is to love the stranger.

00:43:29.920 --> 00:43:31.780
If we don't tell
stories about this,

00:43:31.780 --> 00:43:36.630
not just the jurisprudence of
Scalia, but the man Scalia,

00:43:36.630 --> 00:43:38.320
we're doing a
tremendous disservice.

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:43.060
And while they prop up Ginsburg
and we're silent with Scalia,

00:43:43.060 --> 00:43:43.690
we're just--

00:43:43.690 --> 00:43:45.380
we have ourselves to blame.

00:43:45.380 --> 00:43:47.650
So those are the kind of
things we just love to do.

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:50.570
And we like to connect our
glorious past to the present.

00:43:50.570 --> 00:43:52.570
I don't know if any of
you see the country music

00:43:52.570 --> 00:43:55.990
special by Ken Burns, but I
urge all of you to see it.

00:43:55.990 --> 00:43:57.745
It's absolutely remarkable.

00:43:57.745 --> 00:44:00.400
And what you really feel as
you get closer to country--

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:03.147
and you can tell Burns
had just discovered

00:44:03.147 --> 00:44:04.480
this thing called country music.

00:44:04.480 --> 00:44:07.300
If you recall, his first
documentary on American music--

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:10.060
and he's done maybe 20
by now, but his first one

00:44:10.060 --> 00:44:12.460
was on jazz, because
he's a city guy.

00:44:12.460 --> 00:44:15.760
So he actually thought jazz was
America's music, only it never

00:44:15.760 --> 00:44:18.280
was, because it never
caught on, because it

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:21.230
was too complex, too highbrow
for the average American.

00:44:21.230 --> 00:44:24.310
No, country music and rock and
roll were the American music.

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:25.810
This is what America
latched onto.

00:44:25.810 --> 00:44:27.932
This is what we have
exported to the world

00:44:27.932 --> 00:44:29.140
and we're selling like crazy.

00:44:29.140 --> 00:44:30.090
And what was interesting
about listening

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:32.050
to the country
musicians is they all

00:44:32.050 --> 00:44:36.160
had a deep respect and reverence
for their predecessors.

00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:38.050
They all knew every
Bill Monroe song.

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:40.120
They all knew every
Chet Atkins song.

00:44:40.120 --> 00:44:42.250
And yet they also wanted
to take that country

00:44:42.250 --> 00:44:44.440
music, their
predecessors' great art,

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:46.610
and move it to another place.

00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:50.410
So both loving the formational
character and necessity of what

00:44:50.410 --> 00:44:52.250
brought country to
country, and yet

00:44:52.250 --> 00:44:54.140
how to make America our own.

00:44:54.140 --> 00:44:55.950
So we want to love
George Washington,

00:44:55.950 --> 00:44:57.977
but we want to say that
this is our country now.

00:44:57.977 --> 00:45:00.060
How do we take the principles
of the Constitution,

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:02.650
the rule of law, and
apply it to our life?

00:45:02.650 --> 00:45:04.075
How do we create our culture?

00:45:04.075 --> 00:45:05.700
I don't think
Christians, particularly,

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:07.140
and conservatives
are good enough

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:09.820
in asserting our own
cultural identity.

00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:12.240
We have simply had an
identity by reaction

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:14.160
to the adversary's identity.

00:45:14.160 --> 00:45:18.400
And our identity has become
a reactionary identity.

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:21.810
And that's a shame, because
we have so much, so much,

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:23.820
so much to believe in
and an identity that's

00:45:23.820 --> 00:45:25.170
so much richer than theirs.

00:45:25.170 --> 00:45:26.820
Theirs is basically--
their stories

00:45:26.820 --> 00:45:28.990
are built on lies, in the end.

00:45:28.990 --> 00:45:30.560
And ours are built on truth.

00:45:30.560 --> 00:45:31.940
And there was a last moment--

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:34.740
Wynton Marsalis is sitting there
talking about country music,

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:35.330
and Wynton--

00:45:35.330 --> 00:45:36.900
believe it or not,
this is crazy,

00:45:36.900 --> 00:45:39.020
but an African-American
jazz composer

00:45:39.020 --> 00:45:40.630
and one of our best
musicians is one

00:45:40.630 --> 00:45:43.290
of the regular people talking
through this eight-hour country

00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:44.460
music special.

00:45:44.460 --> 00:45:46.567
And he starts to talk
about Hank Williams.

00:45:46.567 --> 00:45:48.400
And he talks about what
the songs are about.

00:45:48.400 --> 00:45:51.060
He goes, listen to that
lonely [INAUDIBLE] the voice.

00:45:51.060 --> 00:45:52.620
Listen to that pain.

00:45:52.620 --> 00:45:54.330
Listen to that suffering.

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:55.560
My wife did me wrong.

00:45:55.560 --> 00:45:56.700
My husband did me wrong.

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:57.930
I let my God down.

00:45:57.930 --> 00:45:59.550
I didn't let my God down.

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:02.230
God, love, faith, fear.

00:46:02.230 --> 00:46:06.587
We black people, we're always
singing about external stuff.

00:46:06.587 --> 00:46:08.170
Why aren't we talking
about that, too?

00:46:08.170 --> 00:46:10.530
We're all human beings.

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:12.630
This is Wynton Marsalis
condemning his own people,

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:15.030
his own music, for
being superficial,

00:46:15.030 --> 00:46:17.100
for being external,
and for not talking

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:19.525
about our own personal
sins and failings,

00:46:19.525 --> 00:46:21.150
which is what country
music does better

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:23.850
than any other musical
idiom known to man.

00:46:23.850 --> 00:46:26.520
It's a confessional,
most great country music,

00:46:26.520 --> 00:46:28.050
just a pure confessional.

00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:30.810
Most black music is the
protest [INAUDIBLE],,

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:33.120
says nothing about the
person and their sins.

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:36.100
It says and questions
other people's sins,

00:46:36.100 --> 00:46:38.400
which is what Wynton Marsalis
thought made it empty,

00:46:38.400 --> 00:46:39.590
empty music.

00:46:39.590 --> 00:46:40.800
Fascinating.

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:43.443
One last question?

00:46:43.443 --> 00:46:44.110
Yes [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:46.090
AUDIENCE: So can you
[INAUDIBLE] tell us

00:46:46.090 --> 00:46:50.140
what the vision of
Our American Stories

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:51.940
was for you, where
you're at today,

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:54.022
and you hope to be in
the next five years?

00:46:54.022 --> 00:46:54.730
LEE HABEEB: Sure.

00:46:54.730 --> 00:46:58.860
About five years ago, I
was on a conference call--

00:46:58.860 --> 00:47:02.210
I accompanied Bill Bennett,
who of all the people

00:47:02.210 --> 00:47:04.390
I've spent a lot
of time with, Bill

00:47:04.390 --> 00:47:06.380
had the most impact
on both me and Laura.

00:47:09.810 --> 00:47:11.970
Laura's first job
was for Bill Bennett

00:47:11.970 --> 00:47:16.000
in the Reagan administration,
writing speeches for Bill.

00:47:16.000 --> 00:47:19.540
And Bill just has a way of
mentoring people and demanding

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:20.500
that they read.

00:47:20.500 --> 00:47:21.803
He wants us to read.

00:47:21.803 --> 00:47:23.470
And it's always, did
you read that book?

00:47:23.470 --> 00:47:24.190
Did you read that book?

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:25.390
And he makes you feel like--

00:47:25.390 --> 00:47:26.890
I mean, even if you
read a lot of books,

00:47:26.890 --> 00:47:29.367
he has this routine ability
to make you feel like a moron.

00:47:29.367 --> 00:47:29.950
But it's good.

00:47:29.950 --> 00:47:31.325
He doesn't do it
condescendingly.

00:47:31.325 --> 00:47:33.400
He's challenging you all
the time to read more.

00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:37.330
So he has this speech that
is a typical stump speech.

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:39.650
But on this particular
day, Bill's really grumpy.

00:47:39.650 --> 00:47:43.390
So he's speaking to the
Florida 100, like, the movers

00:47:43.390 --> 00:47:44.640
and shakers of Florida.

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:45.970
And he just goes off script.

00:47:45.970 --> 00:47:48.335
And he gives a talk about
the American story and how

00:47:48.335 --> 00:47:49.460
we're losing the narrative.

00:47:49.460 --> 00:47:50.920
And I mean, it was just--

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:52.220
he was not happy.

00:47:52.220 --> 00:47:54.430
By the way, the
audience was mesmerized.

00:47:54.430 --> 00:47:56.668
But I'm sitting there,
and every once in a while,

00:47:56.668 --> 00:47:57.460
he's looking at me.

00:47:57.460 --> 00:47:59.110
Then the next day,
well, we have what

00:47:59.110 --> 00:48:01.150
we call our Salem
editorial meetings, which

00:48:01.150 --> 00:48:04.100
is when all the hosts and
some outside writers join,

00:48:04.100 --> 00:48:05.650
Bill's at it again.

00:48:05.650 --> 00:48:07.310
Are we moving the
needle, he kept

00:48:07.310 --> 00:48:10.300
asking us, especially some of
his senior producers and vice

00:48:10.300 --> 00:48:10.980
presidents.

00:48:10.980 --> 00:48:12.520
Are we changing anybody?

00:48:12.520 --> 00:48:14.730
We do an interview-- what
he was really saying was,

00:48:14.730 --> 00:48:16.760
are we bringing new
people to the choir?

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:18.740
Are we growing market share?

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:20.050
And I think he knew the answer.

00:48:20.050 --> 00:48:20.800
We weren't.

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:23.622
And in some ways, it was
just about at that time

00:48:23.622 --> 00:48:25.330
when the chorus, the
conservative chorus,

00:48:25.330 --> 00:48:27.280
was starting to pick at itself.

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:29.080
So we weren't just
growing the choir.

00:48:29.080 --> 00:48:32.570
We're actually sort of dividing
and splitting up the choir.

00:48:32.570 --> 00:48:35.600
RINOs, Tea Party, I mean, it
was just getting to that point

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:37.980
where we were all just--
we were killing each other.

00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:40.180
And so I took it personally.

00:48:40.180 --> 00:48:41.530
I really listened to him.

00:48:41.530 --> 00:48:44.290
And so the next
day I just thought,

00:48:44.290 --> 00:48:45.820
Bill, what's that all about?

00:48:45.820 --> 00:48:47.410
And he said, you
know, what would

00:48:47.410 --> 00:48:50.267
This American Life, the NPR
product, which he loved,

00:48:50.267 --> 00:48:51.850
he said, what would
This American Life

00:48:51.850 --> 00:48:55.056
sound like if the people
making it liked their country?

00:48:55.056 --> 00:48:56.903
It was a damn good question.

00:48:56.903 --> 00:48:58.320
And that just,
that was it for me.

00:48:58.320 --> 00:49:00.240
I went to Ed Atsinger, our
CEO, the head of Salem.

00:49:00.240 --> 00:49:02.350
I said, look, boss, I'm going to
have to quit in the next year,

00:49:02.350 --> 00:49:04.058
because I've got this
thing I want to do.

00:49:04.058 --> 00:49:05.058
And he said, don't quit.

00:49:05.058 --> 00:49:05.770
Run these shows.

00:49:05.770 --> 00:49:06.960
Just make this be another show.

00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:07.750
Go do what you do.

00:49:07.750 --> 00:49:08.710
Step it up.

00:49:08.710 --> 00:49:09.858
It's a nonprofit.

00:49:09.858 --> 00:49:12.400
And I went to [INAUDIBLE] who's
the founder of the Federalist

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:13.240
Society.

00:49:13.240 --> 00:49:14.590
I share with him the vision.

00:49:14.590 --> 00:49:16.170
I went to Dr. Arnn.

00:49:16.170 --> 00:49:17.340
I share with him the vision.

00:49:17.340 --> 00:49:18.715
And then I went
to Bernie Marcus,

00:49:18.715 --> 00:49:20.950
the founder of Home
Depot, and Phil Anschutz,

00:49:20.950 --> 00:49:23.013
a really remarkable
Christian philanthropist.

00:49:23.013 --> 00:49:25.180
And they both gave me the
seed capital, because they

00:49:25.180 --> 00:49:26.510
said, no, build that.

00:49:26.510 --> 00:49:27.050
Build it.

00:49:27.050 --> 00:49:28.090
So what we're doing
now is building

00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:29.673
a two-hour a day
national storytelling

00:49:29.673 --> 00:49:32.218
show, on radio, because that's
still where the people are.

00:49:32.218 --> 00:49:34.135
And then ultimately one
day it'll be podcasts.

00:49:34.135 --> 00:49:36.677
And what we hope to do is build
enough intellectual property,

00:49:36.677 --> 00:49:40.160
enough stories about
all kinds of Americans,

00:49:40.160 --> 00:49:43.110
that one day we can go to a
Netflix or somebody like it

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:44.985
and say, hey, we've got
hundreds and hundreds

00:49:44.985 --> 00:49:46.350
of hours of content.

00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:47.770
We've got a whole lot of fans.

00:49:47.770 --> 00:49:49.390
The stories have been written.

00:49:49.390 --> 00:49:51.100
And we'd like to just film them.

00:49:51.100 --> 00:49:52.840
I'd also love-- the
vision would be--

00:49:52.840 --> 00:49:54.215
to have a bunch
of the people who

00:49:54.215 --> 00:49:55.810
work for me, at one
day in the future,

00:49:55.810 --> 00:49:58.480
say, hey, I want to
start my own show.

00:49:58.480 --> 00:50:00.070
I want to start my own thing.

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:02.500
And then I will have
done the best service

00:50:02.500 --> 00:50:04.600
to my donors, which
is to train up

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:07.480
a new generation of executives,
because this is a business.

00:50:07.480 --> 00:50:09.730
And we heard this in the
country special all the time.

00:50:09.730 --> 00:50:11.560
Country music is business.

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:15.340
If you don't sell records,
then you don't have a career.

00:50:15.340 --> 00:50:17.948
And then if you don't go
tour, you don't have a career.

00:50:17.948 --> 00:50:19.240
The left's really good at this.

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:21.880
They take this business
really seriously.

00:50:21.880 --> 00:50:26.020
And that's the plan, is to
just go do something big

00:50:26.020 --> 00:50:27.760
and have a big cultural impact.

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:29.530
We try not to be on
talk radio stations.

00:50:29.530 --> 00:50:31.060
We want to be on
country stations.

00:50:31.060 --> 00:50:34.212
We want to be on
classic rock stations.

00:50:34.212 --> 00:50:36.420
And then when somebody hears
the story of Henry Ford,

00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:37.840
they're going to
go, my goodness,

00:50:37.840 --> 00:50:41.260
I did not know that he
got wealthy lowering

00:50:41.260 --> 00:50:44.710
the price of cars
and that cars, which

00:50:44.710 --> 00:50:48.500
were hereto not available
to even kings 50 years ago,

00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:50.740
and then were only
available to the rich,

00:50:50.740 --> 00:50:52.640
were now available to everybody.

00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:54.960
And we explain
capitalism that way,

00:50:54.960 --> 00:50:58.060
that nothing else can lower
the price of something

00:50:58.060 --> 00:51:01.960
and make it better and
cheaper than capitalism can,

00:51:01.960 --> 00:51:04.870
through competition,
through free enterprise,

00:51:04.870 --> 00:51:07.930
and through, in the end,
consumers choosing who wins

00:51:07.930 --> 00:51:10.270
and who loses, not by
force, but by choice.

00:51:10.270 --> 00:51:12.020
And by the way, this
resonates with people

00:51:12.020 --> 00:51:15.220
when I talk to kids in schools
and I talk to them about life

00:51:15.220 --> 00:51:16.120
as it is.

00:51:16.120 --> 00:51:16.890
I said, you know--

00:51:16.890 --> 00:51:19.930
one day I played a
clip of Barack Obama.

00:51:19.930 --> 00:51:21.850
And Obama had said
in 2009 what we

00:51:21.850 --> 00:51:24.700
need to do is take all
the insurance companies,

00:51:24.700 --> 00:51:26.740
get rid of the
profits of each one,

00:51:26.740 --> 00:51:28.570
get rid of the
market in each one,

00:51:28.570 --> 00:51:30.700
fold them into one
enterprise, and then we'll

00:51:30.700 --> 00:51:32.020
be more efficient.

00:51:32.020 --> 00:51:34.120
And I went, anybody
here think there's

00:51:34.120 --> 00:51:35.890
a problem with
what he just said?

00:51:35.890 --> 00:51:37.370
They didn't really flush it out.

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:39.130
I said, now, think
about your own lives.

00:51:39.130 --> 00:51:40.840
Think about flat-screen TVs.

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:43.440
And I put a picture up of
the first flat-screen TV.

00:51:43.440 --> 00:51:46.610
And the first flat-screen
TV cost, like, $15,000,

00:51:46.610 --> 00:51:48.680
and it weighed,
like, 8,000 pounds.

00:51:48.680 --> 00:51:49.910
And three rich guys had it.

00:51:49.910 --> 00:51:51.430
And it didn't work.

00:51:51.430 --> 00:51:56.067
Now, all I did was flash
forward to flat-screen TVs,

00:51:56.067 --> 00:51:58.400
how much better they've gotten,
how much lighter they've

00:51:58.400 --> 00:52:00.233
gotten, and how much
cheaper they've gotten.

00:52:00.233 --> 00:52:02.150
So I said, now, look at
all the companies that

00:52:02.150 --> 00:52:03.275
are making flat-screen TVs.

00:52:03.275 --> 00:52:04.817
There are a whole
bunch of companies.

00:52:04.817 --> 00:52:05.500
Some succeeded.

00:52:05.500 --> 00:52:07.120
Some didn't.

00:52:07.120 --> 00:52:08.350
Let's look at potato chips.

00:52:08.350 --> 00:52:09.790
I took a picture of
a potato chip out.

00:52:09.790 --> 00:52:11.207
Look at all those
different potato

00:52:11.207 --> 00:52:12.670
chips, all the
different profits,

00:52:12.670 --> 00:52:15.010
all the different marketing,
all the different CEOs.

00:52:15.010 --> 00:52:16.960
What would the potato
chip aisle look

00:52:16.960 --> 00:52:19.390
like if the federal
government made

00:52:19.390 --> 00:52:22.483
one potato chip, the
National Potato Chip Company?

00:52:22.483 --> 00:52:24.400
Do you think those would
be good potato chips?

00:52:24.400 --> 00:52:26.680
Do you think they'd
be cheap potato chips?

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:31.780
What part of your life
doesn't free markets work for?

00:52:31.780 --> 00:52:34.022
And by the way, this
is astounding to them,

00:52:34.022 --> 00:52:35.980
because I'm not telling
you to be a Republican.

00:52:35.980 --> 00:52:37.570
I'm not telling you
to be a Democrat.

00:52:37.570 --> 00:52:39.730
I try not to use
the word capitalism,

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:43.400
because it just means
so many bad things.

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:45.857
But when I just talk about
freedom and their own lives

00:52:45.857 --> 00:52:47.940
and why they choose one
restaurant and not another

00:52:47.940 --> 00:52:50.482
and what would happen if there
was one government restaurant?

00:52:50.482 --> 00:52:51.250
Oh, that's right.

00:52:51.250 --> 00:52:55.030
It's the commissary,
and you don't eat there.

00:52:55.030 --> 00:52:58.520
Crummy food and high prices.

00:52:58.520 --> 00:53:00.915
So again, I'm hopeful about
all these things, because we

00:53:00.915 --> 00:53:01.790
have God on our side.

00:53:01.790 --> 00:53:03.060
We have the right
story on the side.

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:04.720
We have the Constitution
on our side.

00:53:04.720 --> 00:53:05.887
We have history on our side.

00:53:05.887 --> 00:53:07.790
We've just been really
crummy at this thing

00:53:07.790 --> 00:53:09.580
called the [? meaning. ?]

00:53:09.580 --> 00:53:12.160
And if we just dared
to play in this space,

00:53:12.160 --> 00:53:15.272
we'd take 50% market
share in 10 years,

00:53:15.272 --> 00:53:17.230
because we've got 50% of
the country, at least.

00:53:17.230 --> 00:53:18.340
And I actually
think we'd get a lot

00:53:18.340 --> 00:53:19.990
of these centrist
Democrats, too,

00:53:19.990 --> 00:53:21.280
because there's troubles now.

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:22.780
I think that a lot
more people would

00:53:22.780 --> 00:53:25.300
be hospitable to our
ideas if they met us

00:53:25.300 --> 00:53:29.620
first through story rather
than through a politician.

00:53:29.620 --> 00:53:31.950
One last question.

00:53:31.950 --> 00:53:32.620
Yes.

00:53:32.620 --> 00:53:35.040
AUDIENCE: Maybe [INAUDIBLE]
there is not a market for this

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:36.850
and that's why it's done on NPR.

00:53:36.850 --> 00:53:38.370
I'm a music fanatic,
and the kind

00:53:38.370 --> 00:53:40.190
of music that I'm
fanatical about

00:53:40.190 --> 00:53:42.790
is Beethoven and
Brahms and Wagner.

00:53:42.790 --> 00:53:44.400
Now, maybe there's
just no market,

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:45.790
and that's why NPR runs it.

00:53:45.790 --> 00:53:48.470
The price, the tax that
I pay for listening

00:53:48.470 --> 00:53:50.860
to my beautiful music
all day is once an hour,

00:53:50.860 --> 00:53:53.480
I have to listen to five
minutes of really increasingly

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:54.716
disgusting propaganda.

00:53:54.716 --> 00:53:56.216
And then every night
at 7:00, I have

00:53:56.216 --> 00:53:58.120
to listen to an hour of that.

00:53:58.120 --> 00:53:59.770
But that's the tax.

00:53:59.770 --> 00:54:02.930
Is there a market
for doing packaging

00:54:02.930 --> 00:54:06.430
that in another way for
people who love their country?

00:54:06.430 --> 00:54:08.065
LEE HABEEB: Well, I
can tell you this.

00:54:08.065 --> 00:54:10.900
I grew up listening to opera
because my father was Italian.

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:12.953
And so Puccini
played all the time.

00:54:12.953 --> 00:54:14.620
But I would bring my
friends, and they'd

00:54:14.620 --> 00:54:17.640
sort of like Puccini, but then
in came Bruce Springsteen.

00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:19.678
And it couldn't compete.

00:54:19.678 --> 00:54:21.970
And Irving Berlin said this
best, because when he came,

00:54:21.970 --> 00:54:24.462
he said, look, I want to make
classical American music.

00:54:24.462 --> 00:54:26.670
And I think, in the end,
our classical American music

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:29.140
is Copeland and it's Dylan.

00:54:29.140 --> 00:54:31.240
And it just is.

00:54:31.240 --> 00:54:37.510
So I would say that classical,
jazz, NPR can afford it

00:54:37.510 --> 00:54:39.580
because it's subsidized.

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:41.410
And then they can
say it's educational.

00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:44.470
But it's some of the
only places we now

00:54:44.470 --> 00:54:46.510
have classical music
left on the dial.

00:54:46.510 --> 00:54:47.210
And I love it.

00:54:47.210 --> 00:54:48.210
AUDIENCE: It's obsolete.

00:54:48.210 --> 00:54:50.600
LEE HABEEB: But
it's almost gone.

00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:54.730
And only sort of a few
elite neighborhoods have it.

00:54:54.730 --> 00:54:57.760
So it's mostly cities that
play the classical music.

00:54:57.760 --> 00:55:00.840
And then the price you pay for
having your classical music is

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:02.107
having to listen to Fresh Air.

00:55:02.107 --> 00:55:03.940
And so I'm so sorry,
because that's painful,

00:55:03.940 --> 00:55:04.982
to listen to Terry Gross.

00:55:04.982 --> 00:55:06.398
And by the way,
if you've noticed,

00:55:06.398 --> 00:55:07.810
NPR is just global warming now.

00:55:07.810 --> 00:55:12.100
I mean, it is just non-stop
sustainability and LGBTQIA.

00:55:12.100 --> 00:55:13.450
I mean, it just never ends.

00:55:13.450 --> 00:55:14.950
Even Marketplace,
which is supposed

00:55:14.950 --> 00:55:18.122
to be about the economy,
is never about the economy.

00:55:18.122 --> 00:55:19.330
So you can't even make it up.

00:55:19.330 --> 00:55:21.760
But it is what it is.

00:55:21.760 --> 00:55:24.610
NPR is actually a ministry.

00:55:24.610 --> 00:55:25.827
It is a ministry.

00:55:25.827 --> 00:55:26.660
People are donating.

00:55:26.660 --> 00:55:27.880
And that's their church.

00:55:27.880 --> 00:55:31.328
And it's a secular ministry, a
secular progressive ministry.

00:55:31.328 --> 00:55:32.870
And while we were
trying to do is go,

00:55:32.870 --> 00:55:34.040
oh, this will be a ministry.

00:55:34.040 --> 00:55:36.730
Our American Stories
will be a pro-rule

00:55:36.730 --> 00:55:39.160
of law, pro-American
ministry that

00:55:39.160 --> 00:55:41.080
loves free enterprise and God.

00:55:41.080 --> 00:55:43.532
And so my feeling
has always been,

00:55:43.532 --> 00:55:45.740
as Bernie Marcus told me--
and he's one of my heroes.

00:55:45.740 --> 00:55:46.940
He started Home Depot.

00:55:46.940 --> 00:55:49.690
He said, it's so much easier to
be a Lowe's than a Home Depot.

00:55:49.690 --> 00:55:52.500
The guy who comes out and builds
the market goes out there,

00:55:52.500 --> 00:55:54.250
and then Lowe's just
sits around and goes,

00:55:54.250 --> 00:55:56.170
hm, let's just copy
Home Depot and take

00:55:56.170 --> 00:55:57.718
30% of their market share.

00:55:57.718 --> 00:55:59.260
Let's steal some of
their executives.

00:55:59.260 --> 00:56:01.720
And let's, you know, poach
some of their managers.

00:56:01.720 --> 00:56:04.303
And so in the end, here's
NPR doing something,

00:56:04.303 --> 00:56:06.220
and I'm just sitting
there studying it, going,

00:56:06.220 --> 00:56:07.178
what did they do right?

00:56:07.178 --> 00:56:08.140
Let's do it better.

00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:11.560
And let's just sort of
reverse-engineer the stuff

00:56:11.560 --> 00:56:14.120
they do and just do it better.

00:56:14.120 --> 00:56:16.930
So I'm very hopeful, because
innovators, disruptors,

00:56:16.930 --> 00:56:19.390
and entrepreneurs
are just starting

00:56:19.390 --> 00:56:20.860
to enter into this space.

00:56:20.860 --> 00:56:23.320
And if I can inspire
a few, that'll

00:56:23.320 --> 00:56:26.590
be even more important than
making the content itself,

00:56:26.590 --> 00:56:30.150
is inspiring others to
innovate in the space.

00:56:30.150 --> 00:56:31.030
SPEAKER 2: Thank you.

00:56:31.030 --> 00:56:31.947
LEE HABEEB: Thank you.

00:56:31.947 --> 00:56:35.210
[APPLAUSE]

